  {
   "text": "Al Capone ruled Chicago, but income tax put him in prison.",
   "start": 0.0,
   "end": 4.62
  },
  {
   "text": "In 1929, seven rival gangsters were murdered, and newspapers named him Public Enemy Number One.",
   "start": 5.64,
   "end": 12.38
  },
  {
   "text": "Agents turned to income tax, and charged him with twenty-two counts of evasion.",
   "start": 12.98,
   "end": 17.54
  },
  {
   "text": "His own admissions of income became evidence.",
   "start": 18.48,
   "end": 20.56
  },
  {
   "text": "Convicted on five counts, he got eleven years.",
   "start": 21.34,
   "end": 24.16
  },
  {
   "text": "Atlanta, then Alcatraz.",
   "start": 24.82,
   "end": 26.44
  },
  {
   "text": "He died in 1947.",
   "start": 26.94,
   "end": 28.46
  },
  {
   "text": "Right way to stop him?",
   "start": 29.18,
   "end": 30.26
  }
